From 108b25959f778cccbac9c3147201bc6bda039ee3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Spanier <spanier.michael@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 06:27:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/43] usb_webcam: survive unplugging, dead clients and
 shutdown

Unplugging a camera while it streamed crashed the add-on server: the
USB device was deleted while the data pumps still used its
endpoints. CamDevice::Unplugged() now stops new transfers, joins the
pumps and only then lets go of the endpoints. Starting a transfer
and the control requests check that the camera is still there.

A camera plugged in again had no node, and the old node waited for
the old device forever. The node now takes back a camera of the same
name that shows up while its own camera is gone (CamReplug.h, tested
by CamReplugTest), and restarts the stream at the connected size.

When the application was killed, the producer kept sending into the
dead port and the camera stayed taken. A failing SendBuffer() with
B_BAD_PORT_ID now makes the control thread disconnect.

Reading the resolution parameter no longer changes the size of the
running stream.

Stopping took two seconds because the C920 needs that long to answer
SET_INTERFACE after streaming. The idle alternate setting is now
selected in its own thread; whoever needs the interface next waits
for it.

The time source object is created while the media server is still
there. BMediaEventLooper::ControlLoop() uses it without a NULL check,
so a node that never streamed crashed when the services shut down.

Tested on a ThinkPad T470s with a Logitech C920 (046d:08e5): pulled
and plugged in while streaming, also at 1920x1080, the picture came
back after about three seconds. 30 rounds of start, stop, size
changes and kill -9 left no crash and no camera that kept sending.

Signed-off-by: Michael Spanier <spanier.michael@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: claude-opus-5-5
(cherry picked from commit 82cefa286af6d1e74e29ae72f42815a521cb264b)
